  "content": {
    "id": "p81-task-1-4-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "1",
    "prompt": {
      "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
      "version": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p81-task-1-4-shared-instruction",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 2,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "True or False?",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p81-task-1-4-part-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "The value that makes the denominator 0 is never allowed in the solution set.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. A zero denominator makes the original rational expression undefined, so that value must be excluded.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p81-task-1-4-part-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "In $\\dfrac{5}{x+1}\\leq 2$, we can multiply both sides by $x+1$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "False. The sign of $x+1$ is unknown. Multiplication reverses the inequality when $x<-1$. Instead, multiply by $(x+1)^2>0$ on the original domain $x\\ne-1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p81-task-1-4-part-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "If $a,b>0$, then $\\dfrac{a}{bx+c}>1$ becomes $a>bx+c$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "False. The conditions $a,b>0$ do not ensure that $bx+c>0$. For example, $a=b=c=1$ and $x=-2$ give $-1>1$, which is false, while $1>-1$ is true. Multiplying by $(bx+c)^2$ gives the equivalent inequality $a(bx+c)>(bx+c)^2$, with $bx+c\\ne0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p81-task-1-4-part-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Solving $\\dfrac{1}{4-x}<0$ is the same as solving $\\dfrac{1}{x-4}>0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. Since $4-x=-(x-4)$, $$\\frac{1}{4-x}=-\\frac{1}{x-4}.$$ Multiplying by $-1$ reverses the inequality, giving $\\dfrac{1}{x-4}>0$. Both expressions exclude $x=4$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      }
    ]
  },
